@Steve L 

Weird how little snippets of things stick in your brain. “This must be the place” reminded me of “Annie Hall”. Alvie is interviewing with a comedian to write his material, the comedian is talking about how classy he is and is prancing around doing a French accent joke, but he says he always makes his entrance after a stupid song that ends “this must be the place!” It’s a minor little scene from 50 years ago but somehow that phrase evoked it (of course I’ve rewatched it since, but still, not lately…)

Incidentally nice to know I’m not the only one who has to put off the puzzle sometimes for spousal harmony.

@acjones
Skipping over all the discussion, just to say you might want to rethink all of those apostrophes that you're using when you simply want a plural noun or when a verb needs an S.
A little proofreading to neaten up your prose is helpful to readers!  (I have a different "pet mistake" that I check for most of the time.)

@Katie 
So I guess I'm about 30 years too old.  I didn't know any of these and had to get everything from the crosses!

You can tell I'm not much of a pop music buff.  Stopped listening when I graduated from high school lin 1967!

@Andrzej FWIW, I do the daily Spelling Bee in an iOS app called Pangram, so that my wife can do it in the NYT Games app. Pangram allows a much broader array of words, probably because it uses an actual dictionary (slightly expurgated to prevent racial slurs, etc.) and not a word list curated by Sam Ezersky. Pangram also didn’t accept Egyptology. Even my spell checker wants to capitalize it. 

I think it’s considered a proper noun (in English, at least) because it is the study of a specific country/nation/kingdom. It’s not a general science, but the study of a very specific instance. I’m struggling to come up with an example, but it’s the difference between chemistry and the study of a single hydrogen atom - not all hydrogen atoms, but this particular one that we named Jan. I think it would be called Janology. 

But I’m no linguist. These are just my thoughts.

@Andrzej 

How is Egyptology any different from, say, American or Polish History (in that specific regard)?

Also, in a couple minutes of browsing, I haven’t come across a single reference that did *not* capitalize the leading “E”. For example: <a href="https://egyptology.christs.cam.ac.uk/about" target="_blank">https://egyptology.christs.cam.ac.uk/about</a>

